Reject the reserved literal/length symbols 286 and 287.

RFC 1951 section 3.2.6: "Literal/length values 286-287 will never actually
occur in the compressed data, but participate in the code construction."
The fixed Huffman code gives both an 8-bit code, so a fixed block can carry
one. s_length_base and s_length_extra have no entry for them (their slots
hold 0), so tinfl decoded either as a match of length 0, read a distance,
and carried on: an invalid stream decoded to bytes, where zlib's inflate
refuses it with "invalid literal/length code". deflate-raw has no checksum
to catch it, so the bytes came back as data (zukomp #94).

Dynamic blocks cannot reach this: HLIT allows at most 286 codes, so
symbols 286 and 287 never get one there.

The state number, 61, is clear of upstream's and of patch 0004's 60.
Written to be upstreamable as-is.

--- a/miniz.c
+++ b/miniz.c
@@ -2764,6 +2764,15 @@
                     }
                     if ((counter &= 511) == 256)
                         break;
+                    /* RFC 1951 3.2.6: literal/length values 286 and 287 "will
+                       never actually occur in the compressed data". The fixed
+                       code still gives them codes, and s_length_base has no
+                       length for them, so a stream holding one is refused, as
+                       zlib's inflate does ("invalid literal/length code"). */
+                    if (counter > 285)
+                    {
+                        TINFL_CR_RETURN_FOREVER(61, TINFL_STATUS_FAILED);
+                    }
 
                     num_extra = s_length_extra[counter - 257];
                     counter = s_length_base[counter - 257];
